  [PASS] V1a  the bound machinery reproduces independently -- g04's A1a is arithmetically sound   (worst |log10 ratio| vs the committed BOUNDS.md numbers is 0.0087, from a completely separate mpmath implementation with the closed form re-derived from Lagrange's equations rather than copied.  g04's A1a number 0.0087 is CONFIRMED)

  V2 -- but the '<1%' description of that validation is WRONG, and it is a log/percent conflation
  worst |log10 ratio| = 0.0087   ->   as a FRACTIONAL error that is 10^0.0087 - 1 = 2.02%
  worst actual fractional deviation over the six planets = 1.98%  (Mercury, 0.9802 of BOUNDS.md)
  [FAIL] V2a  the claim's stated validation precision '<1%' is correct   (IT IS NOT.  The worst per-planet deviation is 1.98% (Mercury), not <1%.  The '<1%' comes from reading the log10 residual 0.0087 as if it were a fraction; 10^0.0087 - 1 = 2.02%.  g04's own A1a text says 'all within 3%', which is right -- the ERROR IS IN THE CLAIM SUMMARY, not in the script.  Immaterial to the verdict at a 10^320 margin, flagged because it is exactly the kind of number that gets quoted downstream)

  V3 -- THE ESTIMATOR ATTACK: g04's A2 margin uses dg AT r = a against a CONSTANT-dg bound
  Route A's dg(r) = g_N/(exp(sqrt(g_N/a0)) - 1) falls off super-exponentially inward, so along ONE orbit
  it spans many decades and its orbit average is set at APHELION.  Evaluating it at r = a therefore
  UNDERSTATES the observable.  The self-consistent comparison is to push the true dg(r) through the same
  Gauss integral that defined the bound, and compare the precession RATE to sigma(omega-dot) directly.

