    'A2 margin'   = log10(bound) - log10(dg at r=a)                       <- what g04's A2a reports
    'TRUE margin' = log10(sigma_wdot) - log10(|orbit-averaged rate|)      <- apples to apples
    'A2 optimism' = how many decades the point estimate is too generous by
  [FAIL] V3a  g04's A2 margins are the right estimator -- the point value at r = a is not systematically low   (THEY ARE NOT the right estimator.  Comparing dg at r = a against a bound built for a CONSTANT dg is a mismatch, and it runs in the ANTI-CONSERVATIVE direction on every planet and both footings: the optimism ranges from 17 to 1522 decades, worst on Mercury (highest e times highest sqrt(y), so the aphelion/mean contrast is largest).  Every A2a margin is overstated)
  [FAIL] V3b  the claim's quoted margin range '10^320 to 10^8900' is the correct range   (IT IS NOT.  Done self-consistently the range is 10^303 to 10^7397, against the claimed 10^320 to 10^8918.  The smallest margin (Saturn, alt footing) is 10^303, not 10^320.  THE NUMBERS IN THE CLAIM ARE WRONG; the CONCLUSION they support is not, and the next check says so)
  [PASS] V3c  the qualitative conclusion nevertheless survives the correction: absolute, not marginal, discharge   (the smallest self-consistent margin over six planets and both footings is still 10^303.  A ~1522-decade correction to a ~303-decade margin changes nothing about whether the isotropic tail is discharged.  The claim's ADJECTIVE ('absolutely, not marginally') is right; its DIGITS are not)

